"Internal Error ()\nciteturn41218view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23382634\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: URL https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23382634 is not accessible via this tool.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn41218view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22820v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22820v1: Cache miss\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMachine Learning (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.LG/new)\nciteturn41218view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/list/cs.LG/new\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 5005\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8:   * cite5†New submissions L9:   * cite6†Cross-lists L10:   * cite7†Replacements L11: \nL12: See cite8†recent articles\nL13: \nL14: ### Showing new listings for Monday, 19 October 2026\nL15: \nL16: Total of 391 entries\nL17: \nL18: Showing up to 2000 entries per page: cite9†fewer | more | all\nL19: ### New submissions (showing 126 of 126 entries)\nL20: \nL21: [1] cite10†arXiv:2610.22379 [cite11†pdf , cite12†html , cite13†other ]\nL22: \nL23: \nL24: Title: When is a large language model a good calibration agent? Physics-labeled prompting for budgeted engineering simulator calibration\nL25: \nL26: cite14†Ruo-Qian Wang L27: \nL28: Comments: Submitted to Engineering Application of Artificial Intelligence\nL29: \nL30: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL31: Calibrating expensive engineering simulators requires tuning model parameters to match reference data, a process heavily constrained by computational costs. This study investigates whether Large Language Models (LLMs), conditioned on physical diagnostic descriptions of simulation errors, can propose informative parameter updates more efficiently than conventional derivative-free optimizers. We test this on a cylinder-wake Large-Eddy Simulation and a damped-oscillator inverse problem.\nL32: Physics-labeled prompts are evaluated against four text ablations (removed, anonymized, padded, misdirected) and standard optimizers (Nelder-Mead, Bayesian optimization, CMA-ES, and random search). Results demonstrate a strong early semantic advantage: the first physics-informed LLM proposal improves the initial design in 23 of 25 trials. After five evaluations, the median calibration error is 21.9 times lower than physics-free controls.\nL33: However, this advantage is transient - disappearing by fifteen evaluations - and inherent LLM decoder variability yields a 9.8-fold performance spread. To address this, we introduce a hybrid workflow that uses the LLM as a warm-start, passing accumulated evaluations to a conventional optimizer. This two-stage numerical continuation policy reaches target accuracy in just 9 simulator runs, outperforming the LLM alone (12 runs), Nelder-Mead (17 runs), and Bayesian optimization (>60 runs).\nL34: Supported by sensitivity analysis validating the test cases, this hybrid physics-labeled LLM scheme proves highly effective as a calibration agent under strict evaluation budgets.\nL35: [2] cite15†arXiv:2610.22380 [cite16†pdf , cite17†html , cite18†other ]\nL36: \nL37: \nL38: Title: Trust but Verify: Designing Region-Inhomogeneous Forward Dynamics for Discrete Diffusion\nL39: \nL40: cite19†Gunmay Jhingran L41: \nL42: Comments: Accepted to 40th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2026). Workshop: AI for Stochastic Dynamics. 10 pages\nL43: \nL44: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL45: Continuous diffusion treats the forward process as a design space; discrete language diffusion often uses absorbing corruption, leaving context fully trusted throughout generation. This makes trust binary: corrupted context is copied as ground truth, propagating errors into generated spans. We reopen this design space by combining absorbing and uniform-mixing kernels with region-dependent rates.\nL46: Missing positions follow absorbing dynamics, while context undergoes low-rate random substitution, exposing the model to observed but uncertain tokens during training. At inference, a heuristic sampler re-injects noise into suspicious context under an annealed intensity and freezes context updates late in the trajectory. An 8,000-step finetune of a pretrained 170M-parameter masked diffusion model takes under five hours on a 6 GB laptop GPU.\nL47: The base model assigns roughly 50% probability to observed tokens even when they are uniform-random garbage. For uniform-random corruption, finetuning detects corrupted context with 0.96 recall and 0.91 precision, improves infill token-F1 by 3.8 points at 10% corruption and 6.4 points at 20%, and removes the accuracy dip next to corruptions. Clean conditioning shows no measurable regression, with a false-edit rate below 0.4%.\nL48: Gains for plausible corruptions are weaker: about 0.5 F1 points at 10% corruption, without statistical significance. Our results suggest that detectability and damage depend jointly on departure from the data conditional: nearby noise is hard to detect and semantically mild, while distant noise is conspicuous and destructive. We interpret absorbing diffusion as the infinite-contrast limit of a trust axis, analogous to the signal-to-noise gap between known and unknown regions in diffusion inpainting.\nL49: In our setting of a 170M model and a single text domain, intermediate trust is reached by changing forward dynamics rather than architecture.\nL50: [3] cite20†arXiv:2610.22381 [cite21†pdf , cite22†html , cite23†other ]\nL51: \nL52: \nL53: Title: Binding-Specific Relearning Savings for Test-Time Continual Learning\nL54: \nL55: cite24†Zhe Zhou L56: \nL57: Comments: Accepted at the NeurIPS 2026 Workshop on Towards Test-Time Continual Learning Agents (TTCL). 15 pages, 2 figures. Code: cite25†this https URL†github.com L58: \nL59: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL60: When a fact becomes harder to elicit from a language model, has the model lost it, or has it merely become less accessible? Parameter-updating test-time continual learning agents are usually evaluated by current output accuracy; we ask whether that measurement captures how prior experience shapes later updates. We test whether learning a specific factual binding produces a later relearning advantage beyond familiarity with its entity, answer vocabulary, format, or query slot.\nL61: In Qwen2.5-1.5B, we compare three matched, counterbalanced histories: LEARNED receives the correct binding, CONTROL receives matched exposure without it, and WRONG receives a competing relation-valid answer in the same slot. After an identical rehearsal-free continual fine-tuning stream, LEARNED reacquires the target faster than CONTROL at a prespecified checkpoint of partial forgetting.\nL62: WRONG shows no positive advantage over CONTROL, while its comparison with LEARNED provides slot-matched corroboration, subject to possible competitor interference. The advantage remains positive after adjustment for measured pre-relearning behavior. In a secondary descriptive conversion using an unpaired never-seen pool, prior learning reduces the optimizer updates required for restoration by about 18%.\nL63: Current behavioral accuracy can therefore underestimate the lasting effect of previously acquired experience. Code is available at cite25†this https URL†github.com L64: [4] cite26†arXiv:2610.22430 [cite27†pdf , cite28†html , cite29†other ]\nL65: \nL66: \nL67: Title: CGDT-CESN: Correlation-Guided and Dynamics-Trained Clustered Echo State Network for Multivariate Time-Series Prediction\nL68: \nL69: cite30†Hariharan Srinivasan , cite31†Masoom Bhargava , cite32†Sayan Gupta L70: \nL71: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG); Adaptation and Self-Organizing Systems (nlin.AO)\nL72: Accurate prediction of multivariate time series requires models that simultaneously capture variable-specific dynamics and cross-variable interactions. In this work, a Correlation-Guided and Dynamics-Trained Clustered Echo State Network (CGDT-CESN) is proposed, which comprises a modular reservoir architecture with each cluster distinctly specializing in one of the variables in the multivariate time series.\nL73: An innovative reservoir design is proposed in which inter-cluster connections are enabled by empirically observed correlations among variables, providing a data-driven coupling mechanism. Apart from conventional readout training, inter-cluster connection weights informed by correlations are trained, which in turn allows the reservoir to capture time-varying cross-variable dependencies without sacrificing computational efficiency and further improves forecasting accuracy.\nL74: The prediction performance of CGDT-CESN is found to be superior to that of the conventional ESN when evaluated on two fundamentally different datasets: Twitter information cascade evolution and solar wind forecasting. Also, the improvement in performance is preserved across multiple prediction horizons, even when presented with a low volume of data.\nL75: By unifying cluster-specific specialization with learned inter-variable coupling, CGDT-CESN offers a principled, data-driven route to multivariate time series prediction.\nL76: [5] cite33†arXiv:2610.22433 [cite34†pdf , cite35†html , cite36†other ]\nL77: \nL78: \nL79: Title: BOLT: Multi-Bottleneck Bound-Guided Learning for Flexible Job-Shop Scheduling with Time Lags and Resource-Coupled Transport\nL80: \nL81: cite37†Ziheng Zhang , cite38†Wei Zhang , cite39†Yunyi Zhao , cite40†Zuming Liu , cite41†Wei Zhou , cite42†Boon Ping Gan L82: Comments: 12 pages main text plus 18 pages of supplementary material appended after the references (30 pages total). Code, data, and trained models: cite43†this https URL†github.com . Submitted to IEEE Transactions on Automation Science and Engineering\nL83: \nL84: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG); Optimization and Control (math.OC)\nL85: Flexible job-shop scheduling becomes resource-coupled when a scarce vehicle fleet moves jobs between stations, because the schedule sets the transport waits. BOLT (bound-oriented learning for transport-coupled scheduling) builds a lower bound from chain, machine-capacity, and fleet-capacity relaxations. The bound equals the makespan at termination, so its per-step changes form a reward that sums to a constant minus the makespan.\nL86: Because it never exceeds the attainable makespan, each candidate's bound increase is a proven rise of the makespan floor, which the policy reads as a price. The root bound certifies a completed schedule's suboptimality gap without a solver. On industry-motivated 10-module prefabricated-construction instances, explicit transport coupling outperforms a transport-penalty policy in every tested cell where loaded moves occupy at least 66% of fleet time and in none at or below 56%.\nL87: Across five zero-shot 50- and 80-module production batches, BOLT outperforms the best dispatching-rule pair on four and the penalty policy on the three with long moves. In the three-vehicle 80-module batch, its mean makespan is 13.0% below the best pair and 4.2% below an hour-budget constraint-programming solver whose proven gap averages 34%. BOLT decides in 2.3-5.9 ms per event on one processor core and re-plans a disruption in seconds.\nL88: [6] cite44†arXiv:2610.22435 [cite45†pdf , cite46†html , cite47†other ]\nL89: \nL90: \nL91: Title: TRACE: Transformer Residual Activation Codebook Encoding for Communication-efficient Distributed Edge Inference\nL92: \nL93: cite48†Muhammad Azlan Qazi , cite49†Alexandros Iosifidis , cite50†Qi Zhang L94: \nL95: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)\nL96: Distributed Transformer inference across co-located edge devices is fundamentally communication-bound, as exchanging activations between sequence-parallel partitions dominates inference latency under commodity edge bandwidths. Existing compression schemes reduce this cost but retain a structural bottleneck: the received data must still undergo key/value projection before attention can proceed, serializing computation behind communication.\nL97: This paper presents Transformer Residual Activation Codebook Encoding (Trace), a framework that addresses these limitations. Trace places a Vector Quantizer (VQ) at the post-attention residual of every Transformer layer and precomputes codebook-indexed key/value representations.\nL98: During inference, exchanged activations are encoded into compact codebook indices, allowing receivers to directly recover keys and values through table lookup instead of executing the downstream computations required to derive them. By eliminating the received data downstream computation, Trace enables communication--computation overlap and reduces a key latency bottleneck in distributed Transformer inference.\nL99: A hierarchical clustering further structures the learned codebook into a multi-resolution hierarchy, enabling runtime adaptation of the communication bitrate without retraining. The framework supports two operating variants: one prioritizing accuracy and the other balancing accuracy and efficiency through buffer-compatible quantization.\nL100: Experiments on ViT-Base (ImageNet-1K and CIFAR-100) and ViViT-B (UCF-101) show that Trace achieves up to 1.89% higher accuracy and up to 1.27x end-to-end inference speedup compared with the state-of-the-art VQ-based distributed inference framework. Compared with uncompressed sequence-parallel inference, Trace reduces communication overhead by up to 22,624x.\nL101: [7] cite51†arXiv:2610.22436 [cite52†pdf , cite53†other ]\nL102: \nL103: \nL104: Title: Generative adversarial networks with Monte Carlo dropout uncertainty quantification for structural health monitoring: Fibre Bragg grating data augmentation comparison in Hawk T1A aircraft\nL105: \nL106: cite54†Alvaro Peiro , cite55†Marios Impraimakis L107: \nL108: Comments: 18 pages; 18 figures, published paper\nL109: \nL110: Journal-ref: Alvaro Peiro, Marios Impraimakis, Aerospace Science and Technology, Volume 180, Part 5, 2027, Article 114028\nL111: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG); Signal Processing (eess.SP)\nL112: The signal generation capabilities of a novel data augmentation framework are examined in aerospace structures. First, the framework learns class-conditional temporal and spectral characteristics of the measured structural responses. It uses conditional generative adversarial network training and Monte Carlo dropout uncertainty quantification as a Bayesian approximation. This step enables stochastic signal synthesis and uncertainty quantification.\nL113: Second, the generated responses are rigorously compared against experimental measurements across time, frequency, uncertainty, variability, and feature domains to assess physical realism, statistical consistency, and distributional fidelity. Finally, the synthetic data are evaluated to determine whether they should be incorporated into a supervised damage classification workflow.\nL114: The framework is validated on the full-scale benchmark dataset from a BAE Systems Hawk T1A aircraft tested at the University of Sheffield's Structural Dynamics Laboratory for Verification and Validation. The test involves acceleration, fibre Bragg grating strain, and nonlinear dynamic response measurements.\nL115: The results demonstrate that the model accurately captures dominant structural dynamics, it guides on signal use for classification improvement based on uncertainty characteristics, and it provides meaningful uncertainty estimates linked to signal complexity and excitation characteristics.\nL116: Importantly, the framework offers a realistic generative modelling plan as a robust digital twin component for data-driven structural health monitoring, supporting resilient decision-making for condition assessment in safety-critical aerospace systems.\nL117: [8] cite56†arXiv:2610.22437 [cite57†pdf , cite58†other ]\nL118: \nL119: \nL120: Title: CF-NODE: Counterfactual Spatial Node Intervention for Test-Time Adaptation Under Domain Shift, Evaluated Across Five Diabetic Retinopathy Datasets\nL121: \nL122: cite59†Sabarish G , cite60†Deepak Parashar L123: \nL124: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG); Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)\nL125: Automated fundus-based diabetic retinopathy (DR) grading can substantially expand screening capacity, but source-trained models degrade sharply across different cameras, clinics, and patient populations. Existing test-time adaptation methods depend on entropy minimization or target information accumulated from image batches and streams. These mechanisms can reinforce confidently incorrect predictions and break down when clinical images arrive independently.\nL126: We introduce CF-NODE, a novel counterfactual test-time adaptation framework for cross- domain DR grading. Instead of trusting prediction confidence or estimating a target distribution, CF-NODE asks how each prediction responds under a controlled intervention on the evidence supporting it.\nL127: CF-NODE ranks internal spatial nodes using model-derived attention, feature magnitude, and node-set similarity, progressively attenuates the highest-ranked nodes, and uses the stability of the resulting expected-grade response to apply a bounded calibration of the predicted grade distribution. The framework is fully frozen and adapts every image independently using only geometric views and a fixed source-derived prior, requiring no target-derived information or model updates.\nL128: We evaluate CF-NODE on five external DR datasets under repeated-seed evaluation against TENT, EATA, SAR, and T3A. CF-NODE outperforms all four methods with statistically significant improvements over the second-best method, improves AUC on every target domain, and attains the highest average AUC and macro-F1, delivering stronger grade discrimination together with more balanced recognition across DR severity levels.\nL129: These results demonstrate that counterfactual intervention sensitivity provides consistent and deployment-ready domain robustness for single-image clinical inference.\nL130: [9] cite61†arXiv:2610.22438 [cite62†pdf , cite63†html , cite64†other ]\nL131: \nL132: \nL133: Title: Rank Measures of Fine-Tuning Updates Need a Noise Baseline\nL134: \nL135: cite65†Muhammad Usman Altaf L136: \nL137: Comments: 24 pages, 6 figures, 15 tables. Under review at TMLR\nL138: \nL139: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL140: Studies of what fine-tuning does to a language model often summarise the weight update $\\Delta W$ by its rank. We compare the rank measures used for this against the same measures on a random matrix of the same shape, and against two harder references: the pretrained weights, and matched fine-tunes without alignment content. Measures that count directions put our fine-tuning updates only a little below noise; stable rank, computed from the same singular values, puts them far below.\nL141: Both are right: an update has a few strong directions on top of a long tail. The pattern holds for thirteen released pairs up to 8B parameters, and it is not a mark of alignment: with training held fixed, instruction data does not stand out from Wikipedia or code, DPO gives the same update whether its preference labels are real or half swapped at random (one model, modest preference strength), and scrambled text gives the most concentrated update of all.\nL142: Applied to a recent claim that post-training is superficial because the effective rank of the weights barely changes, the baselines show that a random update of the same size leaves that rank almost unchanged while wrecking the model, and that an edit built on the effective rank of the update has no measurable effect in our tests. Rank measures of weight updates need a noise baseline, and claims about alignment need a control without alignment content.\nL143: [10] cite66†arXiv:2610.22439 [cite67†pdf , cite68†html , cite69†other ]\nL144: \nL145: \nL146: Title: Sparse Autoencoders Can Induce Local Geometry for Mitigating Catastrophic Forgetting\nL147: \nL148: cite70†Kamil Faber , cite71†Katarzyna Filus , cite72†Łukasz Pawela L149: \nL150: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL151: Catastrophic forgetting remains a challenge in supervised continual learning, as adapting to new tasks causes representation drift and loss of previously learned information. This raises the question of which representational changes should be constrained while preserving plasticity. We therefore introduce Sparse Autoencoder Geometric Anchoring (SAGA), which converts a Sparse Autoencoder (SAE) trained at each task boundary into local, low-rank constraints on representation drift.\nL152: Replay examples specify where the constraints apply, while leading right singular directions of the encoder Jacobian specify which changes are penalized. The resulting geometry is input dependent and can be retained without keeping the SAE for subsequent tasks' training. We establish its local relation to latent code matching, define its spectral truncation error, and derive a conditional bound on frozen-readout drift.\nL153: With replay and snapshot history, the method achieves the highest mean Class-IL accuracy and lowest mean forgetting among the evaluated ER, DER++, ER-ACE, and SGD baselines on three pretrained ViT-B/16 benchmarks, with accuracy gains of 12.15, 4.79, and 3.22 percentage points over the strongest conventional baseline on CIFAR-100, CUB-200, and ImageNet-R, respectively.\nL154: From scratch results are more mixed, while rank ablations and geometry controls show that SAE-induced low-rank geometry offers useful stability-plasticity trade-offs.\nL155: [11] cite73†arXiv:2610.22448 [cite74†pdf , cite75†html , cite76†other ]\nL156: \nL157: \nL158: Title: Lean4AgenticRL: Advancing Agentic RL with Verifiable Multi-level Process Rewards\nL159: \nL160: cite14†Ruida Wang , cite77†Gerald Friedland , cite78†Tong Zhang , cite79†Chris Kong L161: \nL162: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL163: Enhancing the agentic capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs) to tackle complex software engineering (SWE) and general terminal-usage problems is a pressing challenge. Current state-of-the-art LLMs often go through large-scale agentic Reinforcement Learning (RL). However, they mostly rely on high-level outcome rewards, with minimal consideration of low-level toolcall and mid-level workflow-stage rewards, which makes the training less effective.\nL164: To overcome this limitation, we propose **Lean4AgenticRL**, which, to the best of our knowledge, is the first framework that grounds multi-level process rewards in the unified Lean4 formalization schema for open-ended agentic RL. **Lean4AgenticRL** first builds a *problem-specific workflow* system that balances the agent's own capability and controllability during execution, laying the foundation for further verification.\nL165: Subsequently, our workflow formalization and multi-level reward annotation methods enable agent behavior verification within a general harness system. It provides multi-level rewards to support more effective agentic RL with the workflow-stage-normalized GRPO algorithm. Using **Lean4AgenticRL**, we train **LAR-9B/27B**, a family of models with enhanced agentic capability. Extensive experiments indicate that our model outperforms the leading model of similar size.\nL166: **LAR-27B** achieves **77.3%** on TerminalBench-2.1. On average, our models outperform the base model by **9.89%** and surpass the second-best model of comparable size we know by **5.07%**. These results indicate the potential of integrating formal methods in enhancing open-ended agentic RL.\nL167: [12] cite80†arXiv:2610.22467 [cite81†pdf , cite82†html , cite83†other ]\nL168: \nL169: \nL170: Title: Progressive Misalignment Can Emerge from Benign Recursive Self-Distillation\nL171: \nL172: cite84†Karim Abdel Sadek , cite85†Federico Di Gennaro , cite86†Fanny Yang L173: \nL174: Comments: 9 pages\nL175: \nL176: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL177: Training models on their own outputs has attracted widespread attention as a possible route to advance AI capabilities. One prominent approach is self-distillation, where a model is trained to match the token-level predictions it would have produced when given access to additional context, such as a cue, feedback, or instruction. However, the safety properties of this training procedure are poorly understood.\nL178: In this paper, we present a surprising safety failure of recursive self-distillation, where each self-distilled model becomes the teacher for the next round of training. We observe that when a safe model is recursively self-distilled on its own (safe) generations while receiving an additional non-harmful cue, it becomes increasingly misaligned across a diverse set of harmful queries; we call this Progressive Misalignment.\nL179: We show this failure mode in both the Qwen3 and Phi-4 model families on SORRY-Bench and StrongREJECT. Progressive misalignment also occurs in a more realistic setup, where the fixed cue is replaced by feedback from a simulated user with benign intent. Using a gradient-based method, we are also able to find benign prompts that lead to progressive misalignment when applying recursive self-distillation, showing that harmful training prompts are not necessary for this failure mode to emerge.\nL180: We hope our work encourages future investigations of the safety properties of AI systems recursively trained on their own data.\nL181: [13] cite87†arXiv:2610.22497 [cite88†pdf , cite89†html , cite90†other ]\nL182: \nL183: \nL184: Title: Generative Value Classifier\nL185: \nL186: cite91†Alexander Swerdlow , cite92†Perry Dong , cite93†Chelsea Finn L187: \nL188: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL189: Q-learning is premised on learning a function to predict the value of a state-action pair, with the goal of using such a function to guide policy improvement. Most approaches learn this function from scratch using scalar or distributional return targets. This discriminative approach leaves another source of supervision for learning representations unused: the actions themselves.\nL190: Rather than directly predict the return of a state-action pair, we ask the reverse question: how likely is the action under each possible return? This perspective provides a critic whose generative component is a return-conditioned policy, which can share the actor's architecture. Training this policy supervises the critic with every action rather than with returns alone, and a squared-error loss anchors the critic's Q-value to the bootstrapped target.\nL191: We evaluate our approach on both offline and online RL tasks and show gains over leading discriminative methods, including distributional RL methods.\nL192: [14] cite94†arXiv:2610.22512 [cite95†pdf , cite96†html , cite97†other ]\nL193: \nL194: \nL195: Title: Predicting Privileged Information Effectiveness for On-Policy Self-Distillation\nL196: \nL197: cite98†Kaijian Zou , cite99†Emiliano Penaloza , cite100†MohammadHossein Rezaei , cite101†Anisha Gunjal , cite102†Mohit Raghavendra , cite103†Lu Wang , cite104†Yunzhong He , cite105†Anas Mahmoud L198: \nL199: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL200: On-policy self-distillation (OPSD) enables large language models (LLMs) to learn from privileged information (PI) without a separate teacher model. However, prior work largely treats PI as a fixed design choice, leaving the effects of its content and construction poorly understood. This challenge is particularly important in multi-turn agentic settings, where trajectories contain actions, observations, and reasoning that can be transformed into PI in many ways.\nL201: We systematically evaluate 16 PI configurations across three multi-turn agentic benchmarks and three student models that vary in scale and reasoning mode. We find that PI effectiveness depends on its content, generation source, and the student model. Information-dense trajectories and PI produced by more capable generators generally yield larger gains, whereas thinking models benefit less consistently.\nL202: To assess PI before training, we compare PI utility with two KL-based metrics: sampled-token reverse KL ($D$) and error-step KL contrast ($\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$). The KL-based metrics track post-training performance more consistently than PI utility, which can be distorted by information leakage. Empirically, selecting each task's PI by $\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$ also improves post-training performance over random selection, most when the PI candidates differ widely.\nL203: Finally, effective PI remains beneficial when combined with GRPO, allowing the combined objective to outperform a strong GRPO baseline. Overall, our results provide practical guidance for constructing and evaluating PI in OPSD training.\nL204: [15] cite106†arXiv:2610.22519 [cite107†pdf , cite108†html , cite109†other ]\nL205: \nL206: \nL207: Title: Learning smooth dynamics via Kinematic Interpolants\nL208: \nL209: cite110†Yiheng (Roger)cite111†Jiang , cite112†Youssef Marzouk , cite113†Avishek Joey Bose , cite114†Aram-Alexandre Pooladian L210: \nL211: Comments: 22 pages, including appendix, 4 figures\nL212: \nL213: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG); Machine Learning (stat.ML)\nL214: Inferring dynamics from real-world experimental data is inherently ambiguous, as many plausible dynamical systems can explain the same positional observations at measurement times. For higher fidelity modeling of applications ranging from single-cell RNA to vortex dynamics and even flocking of birds, scientific observables increasingly also include velocity estimates that serve to resolve this issue.\nL215: However, incorporating this rich information in a scalable manner, free of simulation or unnecessary approximations, has remained a difficult challenge. In this paper, we introduce Kinematic Interpolants (KI), a novel framework that extends the seminal methodology of stochastic interpolants to infer nonlinear dynamics across multiple snapshots in phase space, i.e., observables with position and velocity measurements.\nL216: Empirically, we demonstrate that across several scientific datasets, KI achieves superior reconstruction of the underlying dynamics compared to existing baselines while requiring only a fraction of the training time.\nL217: [16] cite115†arXiv:2610.22539 [cite116†pdf , cite117†html , cite118†other ]\nL218: \nL219: \nL220: Title: Don't Retrain, Remix: Adaptation of Web Agents using Mixture Hypernetworks\nL221: \nL222: cite119†Md Farhan Ishmam , cite120†Minh Pham Dinh , cite121†Kenneth Marino L223: \nL224: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL225: The web is not static or uniform. Websites that serve the same purpose can look and navigate very differently. Even within a single site, successive redesigns can change how familiar features are presented and accessed. Humans adapt to this by recognizing familiar interaction patterns and reusing what they already know. We give web agents a similar ability with WebMix, a mixture hypernetwork that adapts them to unseen interfaces by composing previously trained low-rank adapters.\nL226: WebMix begins with a bank of adapters trained on source interfaces. At deployment, a scout agent explores the target interface and summarizes its observations into a compact manual. A lightweight router uses this manual to predict mixing coefficients over the adapter bank, which combine the existing adapters into a new one for inference. Adapting to a new interface takes only a scouting run and a forward pass, without gradient updates on the target.\nL227: With Qwen and Llama models, we evaluate domain transfer on TimeWarp and WebArena-Lite-v2, and version transfer on TimeWarp. In all three settings, WebMix matches or outperforms in-context learning and LoRA baselines, often matching the average success of specialists trained directly on those interfaces. On TimeWarp domain transfer, it gains about 8 percentage points on average over the best baseline with both models.\nL228: These gains do not come at the cost of seen interfaces, where WebMix keeps its lead, and extend to version transfer. Adapting to a new interface can therefore rely on exploration and composition instead of retraining.\nL229: [17] cite122†arXiv:2610.22543 [cite123†pdf , cite124†html , cite125†other ]\nL230: \nL231: \nL232: Title: On the SPOT: Enhancing Speculative Decoding for Diffusion Language Models via Commit-Order Prediction\nL233: \nL234: cite126†Eliezer Shlomi , cite127†Eliron Rahimi , cite128†Avi Mendelson , cite129†Chaim Baskin L235: \nL236: Comments: Preprint, project page: cite130†this https URL†eliezer318.github.io L237: \nL238: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL239: Diffusion language models (DLMs) promise parallel text generation, but in practice face an unresolved speed-quality trade-off. Exact decoding fills in only one masked position per model pass, while faster multi-token decoding schemes are lossy. Speculative decoding is the standard lossless acceleration for autoregressive models. However, it does not transfer directly to DLMs, which must choose both what to generate and where.\nL240: We show that this order is the missing piece: the model's own predictions from the previous pass already give the right token at 92-100% of positions, but not which position comes next. Based on this observation, we introduce Speculating Positions Order Trajectory (SPOT), which learns to predict this order.\nL241: A 0.62M-parameter model reads only the target's output distributions and proposes a tree of candidate continuations, and the target verifies all of them in a single pass by checking whether each equals its own next state. On the verification side, our analysis shows that prior block-diffusion verification approaches either assume $T=0$ or a sequential proposal interface.\nL242: To support tree-based speculation at any temperature, we propose using coupled-noise match verification for diffusion blocks, following shared-Gumbel-noise coupling approaches. Our comprehensive experiments show that SPOT cuts target passes by up to 6.8x and reaches up to 3.2x the throughput of standard decoding, beating the calibrated and training-free drafting baselines in every task and budget at $T=0$ and $T=1$.\nL243: In addition, we show that the learned drafter transfers across models, demonstrating successful transfer across six different DLMs. Project page: cite130†this https URL†eliezer318.github.io L244: [18] cite131†arXiv:2610.22564 [cite132†pdf , cite133†html , cite134†other ]\nL245: \nL246: \nL247: Title: Deterministic Bounds on Accuracy and Stability of Metric-Space Regression and Classification\nL248: \nL249: cite135†Nina Maria Gottschling , cite136†Jakob Gawlikowski , cite137†Michele Caprio L250: \nL251: Subjects: Machine Learning (cs.LG); Optimization and Control (math.OC)\